    {
      "id": "tP1K4XbiGK7dlgOaZJgDn",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 358.7934292638888,
      "y": 661.0555561111112,
      "width": 693.01953125,
      "height": 625,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#ffec99",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b09",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 606887326,
      "version": 1056,
      "versionNonce": 1078013186,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1794673617842,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Registers: on-chip, fastest. Private to one thread.\nHold the variables a thread uses most often.\n\nShared memory: on-chip, fast, but not as fast\nas registers. Shared by threads of one block, so they\ncan cooperate.\n\nLocal memory (not drawn): private to one thread,\nbut lives inside the global memory, so it is slow. Holds what does or \ncannot fit in the registers.\n\nGlobal memory: off-chip, large, slow. Every thread and the host can\nread and write it.\n\nConstant memory: threads can only read it, and reads are fast.\nThe host writes it. Details come in Chapter 7.\n\nHost = the CPU and its RAM. device = GPU and its memory.\n\nBlock and thread labels are (x, y): colum first, then row.\n\nRule of thumb: where you declare a variable decides who can see it and\nhow fast it is. Keep often used data in registers and shared memory;\ngo to global memory as little as possible. \n",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "center",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Registers: on-chip, fastest. Private to one thread.\nHold the variables a thread uses most often.\n\nShared memory: on-chip, fast, but not as fast\nas registers. Shared by threads of one block, so they\ncan cooperate.\n\nLocal memory (not drawn): private to one thread,\nbut lives inside the global memory, so it is slow. Holds what does or \ncannot fit in the registers.\n\nGlobal memory: off-chip, large, slow. Every thread and the host can\nread and write it.\n\nConstant memory: threads can only read it, and reads are fast.\nThe host writes it. Details come in Chapter 7.\n\nHost = the CPU and its RAM. device = GPU and its memory.\n\nBlock and thread labels are (x, y): colum first, then row.\n\nRule of thumb: where you declare a variable decides who can see it and\nhow fast it is. Keep often used data in registers and shared memory;\ngo to global memory as little as possible. \n",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    }
  ],
